# Overview
#
# A conjunction is a word that joins two words or two clauses, such as
# `and`, `but`, or `because`. A clause is a subject and its verb, such
# as `the tests pass`. A causal conjunction joins a cause to the claim
# that it explains (`the job fails because the disk is full`).
#
# The rule reports `since` and `as` where they mark a cause (`the job
# fails since the disk is full`). `since` also marks time, and `as` also
# marks manner or comparison, so the reader must pick the reading.
# `because` marks only a cause, so the fix writes `because`. The rule
# sits at Recommended, because a `since` that marks time still matches
# after a comma.
#
# Expression
#
# After a comma, `since` counts before a determiner, a pronoun, or a
# proper noun (`, since the lock is held`). A determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a
# name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. Harper's
# part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word class.
# The pattern names the three classes as `DET` (a determiner), `PRON` (a
# pronoun), and `PROPN` (a proper noun).
#
# Without a comma, `since` counts when a present-tense auxiliary, a
# helping verb such as `is`, `has`, or `can`, follows its subject,
# directly or after one word (`since the lock is held`, `Since this is
# rarely needed`). `aux` lists those verbs. The filter narrows the
# finding to `since`.
#
# After a comma, `as` counts only before a subject pronoun (`I`, `you`,
# `he`, `she`, `it`, `we`, or `they`) and a verb that carries a tense
# (`, as it is deprecated`). `subject` and `verb` list the words that
# count.
#
# By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"`
# lets the comma branches cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# Without a comma, a `since` that marks time passes. It names a past
# event (`since the upgrade landed`) or a version (`Since v2.3, the flag
# ...`), so it has no present-tense auxiliary.
#
# `do` and `does` stay out of `verb`, because `, as it does for any key`
# compares.
#
# Related Rules
#
# `needs` and `will` stay out of `aux`, because ProseNoNegation,
# ProseFutureTense, and the Recommended ProseObligation report the words
# after them. `you` and `we` stay out of `subject`, because the Required
# ProseFiller reports the filler `as you can see` and `as we have seen`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 128 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled
# hits marked a cause. In the one misfire, a `since` after a comma
# marked time (`Speaking of history, since its launch`).
expr subject [it, they, this, these, there]
expr verb [is, are, was, were, has, have, can, will]
expr aux [is, are, has, have, can, cannot, does, do]
expr main [((,) [(since [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (as @subject @verb)]), <(since [DET, PRON, PROPN] @aux), since>, <(since [DET, PRON, PROPN] * @aux), since>]
let message "Causal `since` or `as`. Write `because`."
let description "Flags `since` and `as` where they mark a cause, which `because` marks in one reading."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Since v2.3, the flag defaults to true."
allows "The hook skips the key, as it does for any unknown key."
allows "The job runs as root."
